``` Introduction

A percent literal turns a space-separated list of bare words into an Array of String or Symbol literals in one shot, without quoting each one individually. `%string(...)`/`%symbol(...)` preserve each word's own casing exactly as written.
```
@puts %string(boo Hoo COOL)   # [boo, Hoo, COOL]
@puts %symbol(BOO hoo Cool)   # [BOO, hoo, Cool]


``` Forcing casing

Six more variants force every word to a particular casing rather than preserving what was written -- lowercase (`str`/`sym`), Capitalcase (`Str`/`Sym`), and UPPERCASE (`STR`/`SYM`). The result is still a real Array either way.
```
@puts %str(Boo hOO cOOl)   # [boo, hoo, cool]
@puts %Str(boo HOO cOOl)   # [Boo, Hoo, Cool]
@puts %STR(boo Hoo cool)   # [BOO, HOO, COOL]

@puts %sym(Boo HOO cOOl)   # [boo, hoo, cool]
@puts %Sym(boo HOO cOOl)   # [Boo, Hoo, Cool]
@puts %SYM(boo Hoo cool)   # [BOO, HOO, COOL]


``` It's a real Array

Every percent literal is a genuine `Ore::Array`, not just something that prints like one -- `.values` gives the underlying Ruby-side values, and any Array method works on it.
```
letters := %string(a b c)
@puts "letters.values: `letters.values`"        # letters.values: [a, b, c]
@puts "letters.length(): `letters.length()`"    # letters.length(): 3


``` Numbers are allowed too

A percent literal's items don't have to be identifiers -- bare numbers work as well, since both identifiers and numbers can be turned into text. A forced-casing variant only affects letters; digits pass through unchanged.
```
@puts %string(boo 4815 Hoo)   # [boo, 4815, Hoo]
@puts %STR(boo 4815 hoo)      # [BOO, 4815, HOO]


``` Interpolating a Statement

A `` `expr` `` item (see learn/statement_expressions.ore) is evaluated immediately, right where it sits, then folded through the same to_s + casing treatment as every other item -- not deferred the way a bare `` `expr` `` normally is elsewhere.
```
cool := 2342
@puts %string(481516 `cool`)   # [481516, 2342]
@puts %STR(boo `cool`)         # [BOO, 2342]


``` What goes wrong

Anything besides a plain identifier, number, operator, or `` `expr` `` item inside the parens -- a string literal, `[1, 2]`, any other bracketed/quoted expression -- raises `Ore::Invalid_Percent_Literal_Expression`.
```
# %str('foo')  -- raises Ore::Invalid_Percent_Literal_Expression: %str(Ore::String_Expr) expects %str(Ore::Identifier_Expr or Ore::Number_Expr or Ore::Operator_Expr)
